          "title": "Continuous-time linear state-space model",
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice, so the usual Lebesgue-measurable sets on $\\mathbb R$ form a complete measure space with Lebesgue measure $\\lambda$. Every bounded interval is Lebesgue measurable, and any interval is the countable union of its bounded intersections with $[-n,n]$; since the Lebesgue-measurable sets form a sigma-algebra, $I$ is measurable and $\\lambda$ restricts to $I$. Let $\\mathbb F$ be $\\mathbb R$ or $\\mathbb C$, let $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be an interval with $t_0\\in I$, and let $N,m,p\\ge1$ be finite. A continuous-time linear state-space model consists of matrices $A\\in\\mathbb F^{N\\times N}$, $B\\in\\mathbb F^{N\\times m}$, $C\\in\\mathbb F^{p\\times N}$, $D\\in\\mathbb F^{p\\times m}$, an initial value $x_0\\in\\mathbb F^N$, and an input $u:I\\to\\mathbb F^m$ whose real and imaginary coordinates are Lebesgue measurable and integrable on every compact subinterval of $I$. A state trajectory is a function $x:I\\to\\mathbb F^N$ whose real and imaginary coordinates are absolutely continuous on every compact subinterval of $I$ (with absolute continuity defined by the $\\varepsilon$–$\\delta$ condition on finite families of disjoint subintervals), with $x(t_0)=x_0$ and $x'(t)=Ax(t)+Bu(t)$ outside a Lebesgue-null subset of $I$. Its output is $y(t)=Cx(t)+Du(t)$. The term $Du(t)$ is the direct input-to-output term and is separate from the state contribution $Cx(t)$.",
