          "dependency_level": 1,
          "statement": "Let $S$ be a nonempty finite set, let $A(s)$ be a nonempty finite set for each $s\\in S$, and let $D=\\{(s,a):s\\in S,\\ a\\in A(s)\\}$. Here **finite** means bijective with a natural number. Put $S_0=|S|$ and $A_0=\\max_{s\\in S}|A(s)|$. Fix a discount $0\\le\\gamma<1$. Give $S$ and $D$ their discrete sigma-algebras and give $[0,1]$ its Borel sigma-algebra. A **bounded-reward finite discounted Markov decision process** is a probability kernel $K$ from $D$ to $S\\times[0,1]$. At pair $(s,a)$, $K(\\cdot\\mid s,a)$ is the joint law of the next state and the reward; these two coordinates need not be independent. Define the state-transition probabilities and expected rewards by\n\n$$P(s'\\mid s,a)=K(\\{s'\\}\\times[0,1]\\mid s,a),\\qquad r(s,a)=\\int_{S\\times[0,1]}\\rho\\,K(ds',d\\rho\\mid s,a).$$\n\nA **history-dependent behavior** is a sequence of probability kernels from observed-history spaces $H_t$ to the finite action alphabet $\\mathcal A=\\bigcup_{s\\in S}A(s)$, assigning probability zero to actions outside $A(s_t)$ at every history ending in state $s_t$. Here $H_t$ consists of histories $(s_0,a_0,R_1,s_1,\\ldots,a_{t-1},R_t,s_t)$, with discrete sigma-algebras on finite state/action coordinates and the Borel sigma-algebra on each reward coordinate; it has the resulting finite-product sigma-algebra. A **stationary Markov policy** assigns one distribution on $A(s)$ to each state, independently of earlier history. During interaction, after choosing $a_t$ in state $s_t$, the next pair $(s_{t+1},R_{t+1})$ is drawn from $K(\\cdot\\mid s_t,a_t)$.",
          "strategy": "Extend the existing finite-state/action MDP interface to general bounded Borel reward laws while retaining its joint next-state/reward convention.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
