          "dependency_level": 2,
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. In every bounded-reward finite discounted Markov decision process with nonempty finite state set $S$, nonempty finite action sets $A(s)$, and $0\\le\\gamma<1$, the trajectory law of every history-dependent behavior from every starting state exists. Its discounted return is measurable, integrable, and between $0$ and $1/(1-\\gamma)$. For each stationary policy $\\pi$, its value $V^\\pi$ satisfies its Bellman equation. The optimality operator\n\n$$T^*V(s)=\\max_{a\\in A(s)}\\left[r(s,a)+\\gamma\\sum_{s'\\in S}P(s'\\mid s,a)V(s')\\right]$$\n\nis a $\\gamma$ contraction in the supremum norm, meaning $\\|T^*V-T^*W\\|_\\infty\\le\\gamma\\|V-W\\|_\\infty$ for all $V,W:S\\to\\mathbb R$, where $\\|V\\|_\\infty=\\max_{s\\in S}|V(s)|$, and has a unique fixed point $V^*$. This fixed point is the supremum of the values of all history-dependent behaviors and is attained by a deterministic stationary greedy policy. The optimal action value is\n\n$$Q^*(s,a)=r(s,a)+\\gamma\\sum_{s'\\in S}P(s'\\mid s,a)V^*(s'),$$\n\nand $V^*(s)=\\max_{a\\in A(s)}Q^*(s,a)$ with $Q^*(s,a)\\ge0$.",
          "strategy": "Construct the history-dependent path law, use the geometric tail bound for discounted returns, and prove Bellman optimality by finite-horizon induction and contraction.",
          "proof_strategy": "direct",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
